Subject: Cantor Execution Surface Profile

Description: One authoritative Cantor engine exposed first through an embedded command-line interface for Codex and later through a persistent local service, without duplicating semantic implementation or authority.

& [SingleEngineInvariant] is the non-duplication boundary
  + [authoritative_core] is the cantor_core Rust library and its versioned semantic protocol
  + [execution_surface_set] is cantor CLI, persistent Cantor service, and later protocol adapters
  + [semantic_equivalence] is equivalent requests over the same packages, state snapshot, policy, and engine version produce equivalent result and proof envelopes
  + [authority_source] is admitted compiled SOP packages rather than an interface-specific prompt or configuration
  = must: implement query, framing, validation, transition, and proof semantics exactly once in cantor_core
  = must: treat every surface as an adapter around the same typed operations
  - never: create a CLI Cantor and service Cantor with divergent semantic rules

& [CantorCLI] is the native executable surface for Codex, humans, scripts, and fixtures
  + [executable_name] is cantor
  + [initial_mode] is embedded_core
  + [later_mode] is service_client
  + [transport] is process arguments plus standard input, standard output, standard error, and exit status
  + [machine_output] is one versioned JSON result envelope on standard output
  + [diagnostics] is human-readable operational diagnostics on standard error
  + [input_modes] is file, standard input, or explicitly bounded arguments
  + [initial_effect_scope] is read-only semantic operations and local fixture execution
  = must: support non-interactive deterministic invocation
  = must: accept large or sensitive structured input through standard input rather than shell arguments
  = must: keep machine output free of banners, progress text, and diagnostic noise
  = must: produce meaningful stable exit classes for success, invalid request, trust failure, unresolved dependency, policy denial, inference fault, and internal fault
  - never: require Codex to parse prose to determine whether an operation succeeded

& [CantorService] is the later resident semantic-coprocessor surface
  + [candidate_executable_name] is cantord
  + [lifecycle] is a persistent local process
  + [owns] is admitted package residency, compiled indexes, caches, session state, provider pools, faculty activations, junction state, notification cursors, and durable trace coordination
  + [interface] is a local versioned request and response protocol derived from cantor_core operations
  + [access_scope] is local-only by default
  + [concurrency] is bounded isolated sessions over explicitly versioned shared state
  = must: authenticate or otherwise bind local callers before effect-capable operations exist
  = must: isolate session state and bind every response to package, state, policy, and frame digests
  = must: make restart, recovery, migration, and cache invalidation explicit
  - never: become an independent semantic authority separate from cantor_core
  - never: expose a network listener by default

& [CantorProtocolEnvelope] is the shared typed interface
  + [protocol_version] is the request and response schema version
  + [request_uuid] is immutable call identity
  + [operation] is query, inspect, prepare_frame, reflect, validate, resume, status, or a later admitted operation
  + [caller_context] is caller identity, purpose, conversation or job, authority scope, and consequence class
  + [input] is the operation-specific typed payload
  + [expected_package_set] is admitted package identities and digests
  + [expected_state] is optional session and frame version precondition
  + [budget] is traversal, retrieval, time, inference, and output bounds
  + [result] is the typed operation result
  + [proof] is sources, derivations, package identities, frame digest, validation, and trace anchors
  + [continuation] is finish, query, reframe, reset, resume, yield, or stop
  + [fault_set] is structured operational, semantic, trust, policy, and inference faults
  = must: remain identical across embedded CLI, service client, MCP, and testing adapters

& [CodexCLIIntegration] is the first external caller path
  + [invocation] is Codex runs cantor as a bounded local subprocess
  + [request] is a CantorProtocolEnvelope supplied through standard input
  + [response] is a validated JSON envelope read from standard output
  + [next_pass] is Codex uses only admitted result and proof fields as its Cantor reflection context
  + [failure] is nonzero exit status or invalid envelope causes a visible fault rather than silent fallback to invented SOP
  = must: make command construction resistant to shell quoting and injection faults
  = must: set time, output, and recursion limits for each call
  = must: preserve request UUID and trace anchor in the surrounding Codex task record
  - never: let an unavailable CLI be interpreted as permission to fabricate Cantor guidance

& [ExecutionSurfaceProgression] is the implementation order
  + [phase_1_core] is cantor_core implements the bounded in-memory deterministic acceptance path
  + [phase_2_embedded_CLI] is cantor embeds cantor_core for fixtures, inspection, and first Codex subprocess calls
  + [phase_3_service_boundary] is stable protocol tests prove equivalent embedded and client-server behavior
  + [phase_4_resident_service] is cantord owns persistent packages, caches, and sessions
  + [phase_5_CLI_client] is cantor selects embedded or service mode without changing operation schemas
  + [phase_6_MCP_adapter] is a narrow local MCP tool maps directly onto the same protocol
  = must: keep the current one-process proof intact through phase_2
  = must: add the resident service only when measured reload cost, shared state, concurrency, or notification requirements justify it
  - never: block core proof on daemon, IPC, MCP, plugin, or distributed-runtime formation

& [CandidateCLICommandSet] is the non-authoritative command projection
  + [query] is resolve signed records, relationships, operations, and proof
  + [inspect] is display package, record, frame, trace, or certificate state
  + [frame_prepare] is construct and readiness-check an InferenceFrameManifest
  + [reflect] is evaluate a ConceptProposal and return a CantorReflectionPacket
  + [validate] is validate a candidate result, faculty return, effect proposal, or capsule
  + [resume] is continue a digest-bound checkpoint
  + [status] is report engine, protocol, package, and service compatibility
  = may: change command spelling before CLI schema freeze
  = must: keep the underlying operations stable and typed

& [RequirementSet] is the traceable execution-surface collection
  + [CES-001] is implement one authoritative cantor_core shared by all surfaces
  + [CES-002] is provide a non-interactive structured cantor CLI suitable for Codex subprocess calls
  + [CES-003] is use standard input for substantial request payloads and standard output exclusively for machine results
  + [CES-004] is define one versioned CantorProtocolEnvelope across embedded, service, MCP, and test adapters
  + [CES-005] is begin with an embedded one-process CLI and defer the resident service until justified
  + [CES-006] is allow the later CLI to become a thin service client without changing semantic operations
  + [CES-007] is make CLI failure explicit and prohibit fabricated fallback guidance
  + [CES-008] is prove semantic equivalence across execution surfaces

& [SJSProcessingTrace] is the execution-surface justification record
  + [fold_1_subject] is two ways to run Cantor, including a CLI that Codex can call
  + [fold_2_boundary] is one engine with multiple adapters inside; duplicated semantic implementations and authorities outside
  + [fold_3_data_design] is core operation, protocol envelope, request, caller context, result, proof, continuation, fault, mode, and version
  + [fold_4_governance] is local-only defaults, structured I/O, digest binding, explicit failure, semantic equivalence, and deferred effects
  + [fold_5_solution_shape] is core, embedded CLI, stable protocol, resident service, CLI client, and MCP adapter
  + [fold_6_fault_review] is implementation drift, prose parsing, shell injection, stale service state, silent fallback, protocol skew, and premature daemon complexity
  + [fold_7_phase_lock] is embedded CLI belongs to the bounded core path while persistent service and MCP remain gated extensions

